\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


The smooth concordance group $\mathcal C$ consists of oriented knots modulo smooth annuli in $S^3\times[0,1]$, with connected sum as addition and unknot $U$ as zero. A knot is smoothly slice if it bounds a smooth disk in $B^4$, and topologically slice if it bounds a locally flat disk. For a pattern $P\subset S^1\times D^2$, the satellite $P(K)$ uses the preferred longitude; its winding number is the signed homology class in the solid torus. Satellite operations induce functions on $\mathcal C$, not necessarily homomorphisms. Write $\mathcal C_{TS}\leq\mathcal C$ for the subgroup of classes of topologically slice knots. The order of a class is the least positive integer $m$ with $m[K]=0$, if one exists; a nontrivial torsion class has finite order greater than one. A knot $J$ is strongly negatively amphichiral if there is an orientation-reversing smooth involution of $S^3$ preserving $J$ setwise and reversing its orientation. The divisibility condition in part(c) is exactly division by infinitely many positive integers, not division by every integer.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine the structure of $\mathcal C$, with the following specific questions. (a) Does $\mathcal C$ contain a nontrivial torsion class of order other than two, and does $\mathcal C_{TS}$ contain such a class? (b) Does some class of order two in $\mathcal C$ have no strongly negatively amphichiral representative? (c) Does there exist a knot $K$ such that the set $\{n\in\mathbb N:\exists[J]\in\mathcal C,\ [K]=n[J]\}$ is infinite? Part(c) is recorded literally: no nonzero or infinite-order hypothesis is imposed on $[K]$; in particular the unknot satisfies this condition. Likewise a finite-order class is divisible by every positive $n$ coprime to its order. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}\href{https://math.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/surv-295-ruberman-watermarked-author-pdf.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Study the smooth concordance group and its topologically slice subgroup, asking about higher torsion, representatives of order-two classes and the stated infinitely-many-integers divisibility condition.

